//!
//! A typical connection flow through these layers:
//!
//! 1. BTM init ([`fn_80200270`]) sets the timing default and clears the
//!    disconnect reason.
//! 2. The host registers a connection callback ([`fn_80201710`]) and BTM keeps
//!    the device database ([`fn_8020213c`], [`fn_80202460`]).
//! 3. On connect, the HID host claims one of four slots ([`fn_80200310`]); a
//!    completed `bta_hh_sdp_cmpl` setup frees it again through
//!    [`fn_80200000`], and [`fn_80200624`] drops it by `BD_ADDR`.
//! 4. Outgoing HCI commands are built by the `0x8020E55C..0x8020F1F4` family
//!    and queued through [`fn_8020ac78`], which drains the input queue into the
//!    controller queue.
//! 5. L2CAP ([`fn_80212648`], [`fn_80212810`], [`fn_80218040`]) and RFCOMM
//!    ([`fn_80219e40`], [`fn_8021ce04`]) carry the HID control/interrupt
//!    channels above the controller link; [`fn_80217de4`] and
//!    [`fn_802169ac`] tear individual channels down again.
//!
//! # Start here
//!
//! [`fn_8020117c`] is the smallest complete function: it counts how many of the
//! four per-device slots are in use. It introduces the slot table at
//! `0x803D1BAC` (stride `0x11C`) that most of the HID-host code touches. Read it
//! first, then [`fn_8020028c`] (find a slot by id) and [`fn_80200310`] (allocate
//! a slot and run the connect-complete path). For the packet path, read
//! [`fn_8020ac78`] - it is the queue that every builder feeds - then
//! [`fn_8020e55c`] and [`fn_8020e784`] for the packet layout. For channel state,
//! read [`fn_80218040`] (PSM lookup) and [`fn_80217de4`] (channel unlink).
//!
//! # Dependencies
//!
//! - [`crate::platform`] for big-endian volatile reads/writes and for calling
//!   routines that are outside this module.
//! - No other Rust module is imported. These addresses are called through
//!   [`crate::platform::call0`]..[`crate::platform::call5`] and are **not**
//!   translated here:
//!   - `0x800046B4` - `memset` wrapper from the boot lane;
//!   - `0x80004364` - `memcpy` from the boot lane;
//!   - `0x801F7150` / `0x801F7240` - buffer allocator and free;
//!   - `0x801F7670` / `0x801F78F0` - queue append and pop;
//!   - `0x801F7A60` / `0x801F7A70` / `0x801F7990` - queue head, next and unlink;
//!   - `0x801F9B50` - queued-buffer release;
//!   - `0x801FA320` / `0x801FA390` - timer arm/cancel;
//!   - `0x801FAAF0` - heap-block release;
//!   - `0x801F9D30`/`0x801F9DD0`/`0x801F9DB0` - diagnostic loggers;
//!   - `0x80203C4C` - BTM stored-link-key deletion;
//!   - `0x8020D568`/`0x8020D878`/`0x8020D8F4`/`0x8020D970` - HCI link-control
//!     command builders (opcodes `0x040F`/`0x041B`/`0x041D`/`0x041F`);
//!   - `0x80212F34` - L2CAP data-write entry that is still called by address;
//!     the second entry `0x80215CEC` is [`fn_80215cec`] since wave 112;
//!   - `0x80213240` - L2CAP channel event dispatcher, now [`fn_80213240`];
//!   - `0x80218624` - L2CAP connection-start helper (`0x8021843C` is
//!     [`fn_8021843c`] since wave 101);
//!   - `0x802122B0` - HID report fragmenter, now [`fn_802122b0`];
//!   - `0x80230280` - text-anchor measure, translated as
//!     `resource::bres::fn_80230280` and reached here by address;
//!   - `0x8021B738` - RFCOMM event dispatcher;
//!   - `0x802092FC` - "is this handle on a SCO link?" query;
//!   - `0x802DF098` - 6-byte comparator used as `memcmp`.
//!
//! # Classification notes
//!
//! The three utilities at the end of the file were translated because they are
//! complete, self-contained functions in the `0x80200000..0x8025FFFF` lane, not
//! because their subsystem is proven. [`fn_8024d390`] in particular indexes a
//! 128 × `0x44` table whose only caller branches on flag bits inside
//! transform-heavy code; that looks more like animation/collision data than
//! Bluetooth. They stay here as `// unknown:`-marked helpers rather than being
//! silently assigned to a gameplay module. If a later lane proves a better
//! home, move them and update the ledger.
//!
//! Waves 42 and 47 extended the same reasoning to the rest of the
//! `0x80220000` lane. Wave 42 named four clusters but only landed the first
//! two; Wave 47 added the remaining 31 bodies:
//!
//! - the one-method classes at `0x80229360..0x80229820` (4 bodies; vtables
//!   `0x8034CC18` and `0x8034CC2C`),
//! - the 0x16C-byte class and its guarded drain queue at
//!   `0x8022A6B0..0x8022B680` (16 bodies; vtable `0x8034CD10`),
//! - three neighbouring vtable classes at `0x8022BB80..0x8022C340` (6 bodies),
//! - five standalone helpers at `0x80224340..0x80227970`: a word table at
//!   `0xCD000000`, a bounded string length and two size-checking wrappers.
//!
//! None of them is Bluetooth protocol code. They are documented because the
//! image places them in this lane and because the globals they touch are part
//! of the original layout.
//!
//! Wave 53 added 25 bodies to the `0x80200000` bucket, which earlier passes
//! had left entirely untranslated:
//!
//! - fifteen link-control packet builders at `0x8020CC2C..0x8020D970`
//!   (thirteen allocate their own `2`-class buffer, two fill a
//!   caller-supplied buffer, and the longer ones copy the six-byte address in
//!   reverse order),
//! - five HID-host bring-up routines at `0x8020B91C..0x8020BAD4`: the
//!   subsystem init chain, two control-block clears and the ten-entry callback
//!   table installer,
//! - five repage/originate routines at `0x8020F348..0x8020F8B4`: the repage
//!   block initialiser, the originate attempt, the timeout handler, the
//!   address copy and the single-shot SDP registration.
//!
//! Unlike the `0x80220000` utilities these are HID/HCI transport code: every
//! one writes the packet layout consumed by [`fn_8020ac78`] or drives the
//! `bta_hh` state block. Command mnemonics are still unproven (no string names
//! them), so the packet builders document the literal command word only.
//!
//! Wave 58 added 28 bodies to the same `0x80200000` bucket, all of them
//! RFCOMM `PORT` code that earlier passes had left untouched:
//!
//! - eighteen DLC state callbacks at `0x8021C6D8..0x8021D3DC`: two teardown
//!   callbacks that clear/set the RFCOMM-wide control flag, the stop-flag
//!   setter, the port-settings byte copier, the command forwarder, two guarded
//!   builder wrappers, four short UIH frame builders, the shared frame
//!   finisher [`fn_8021d0d8`] and three response builders,
//! - ten port credit/timer routines at `0x8021E484..0x8021EB8C`: the
//!   receive-side checksum check, the retry and port timer arm/cancel pairs,
//!   the 61-entry idle scan, the window-state event forwarder and the two
//!   credit counter updates.
//!
//! Ten further companions of those callbacks (`0x8021C904`, `0x8021C96C`,
//! `0x8021C9DC`, `0x8021CAE4`, `0x8021CBC0`, `0x8021CC64`, `0x8021CD08`,
//! `0x8021CDA0`, `0x8021CDCC`, `0x8021D270`) were left untranslated by wave 58
//! because their exports call `FUN_80219E40()` with no argument list; wave 101
//! recovered that register pair and landed all ten, plus `0x8021E4D0` in wave
//! 107. `0x8021E628` was translated here in wave 58 by reading its callers.
//!
//! Wave 60 added 26 bodies to the `0x80220000` bucket: the SDP service
//! discovery layer at `0x802202D0..0x80223358`, which the earlier passes over
//! this bucket had not touched.
//!
//! - the client PDU machinery at `0x802202D0..0x802211B4`: two UUID-list
//!   builders, the attribute-list encoder, the service-search response
//!   builder, the PDU dispatcher, the handle collector, the fragment
//!   reassembler and the attribute-response parser,
//! - the database and lifecycle block at `0x802213A0..0x80222544`: the
//!   `0x14`-byte service-record allocator, the subsystem init (security
//!   registration, nine-slot L2CAP callback table, PSM registration), the five
//!   L2CAP event handlers, the originate call, the abort helper and the server
//!   request dispatcher,
//! - the CCB pool at `0x80223254..0x80223358`: lookup by CID, the four-block
//!   allocator and the release path.
//!
//! These exports begin with `FUN_802DCD6C()` and end with `FUN_802DCDB8(...)`.
//! Those are the compiler's non-volatile register save/restore thunks, not
//! game calls (the same finding as `src/game/worldmap.rs`); the decompiler
//! folded the two real arguments into the thunk's fake 64-bit result and
//! printed several of these functions as parameterless. Their bodies are
//! translated with ordinary parameters and do not call the thunks.
//!
//! Wave 66 added 30 bodies to the `0x80200000` bucket, continuing the BTM and
//! HCI code that waves 53 and 58 started:
//!
//! - eight HCI connection-mode packet builders at `0x8020DBD0..0x8020E33C`
//!   (opcodes `0x042A`, `0x0801`, `0x0804`, `0x0805`, `0x0806`, `0x0C03`,
//!   `0x0C0A`, `0x0C0D`; six allocate a `2`-class buffer themselves),
//! - nine BTM eSCO/security helpers at `0x802070A8..0x80207818`: the ACL-slot
//!   lookups and removal, the active-link counter, the security application
//!   registration slot pair and the PIN setter,
//! - eight BTM inquiry and mode-control routines at
//!   `0x80204008..0x80204B6C`: the discoverability request builder, its
//!   teardown, the three mode setters, the inquiry-state getter, the
//!   remote-name cancel and the inquiry database reader,
//! - three BTM init helpers at `0x80205B94..0x80205C30`: two reset forwards,
//!   the global reset and the three-slot service registration,
//! - two pool routines at `0x8020FDCC..0x8020FE10`.
//!
//! Wave 68 added 28 bodies in three further clusters:
//!
//! - six HID-host device-table routines at `0x80210100..0x8021254C`: the
//!   release, originate and disconnect paths plus the PSM registration that
//!   wave 66's pool helpers only cleared,
//! - thirteen RFCOMM routines at `0x8021995C..0x8021AAD8`: the send-queue
//!   drain, the frame-size and credit selection, the LCID lookup with its six
//!   dispatch handlers, and the ten-word L2CAP callback block installer,
//! - nine SDP service-database helpers at `0x8021EFA4..0x8021FE24`: record
//!   allocation, lookup by handle, compaction, attribute-range scan and the
//!   recursive attribute matcher.
//!
//! Wave 68 also identifies the `0x404`-byte "connection pool" of wave 66: the
//! region starting at [`BTM_POOL`] is the HID-host device table - sixteen
//! `0x34`-byte records (`0x340` bytes) followed by the owner callback word at
//! `0x803D4B78` and the enable/trace bytes at `0x803D4C38`/`0x803D4C39`.
//!
//! Wave 69 added 29 bodies to the `0x80220000` bucket, again outside Bluetooth:
//! the two platform drivers that sit between the game and the Hollywood IOS
//! co-processor.
//!
//! - nine arena/IPC bootstrap routines at `0x802241B4..0x80224890`: the cached
//!   OS arena bounds, the two heap creations, the record matcher and the
//!   adjacent-pair sorter,
//! - eight IPC request builders at `0x80224C70..0x80225330` (kinds `1..5`,
//!   `0x40`-byte blocks, MEM2 pointer converted to a bus address, submitted
//!   through `0x80224910`),
//! - five callback-carrying wrappers at `0x80227810..0x80227AE0` (a
//!   `0x140`-byte completion block each, shared callback `0x802265C0`),
//! - five EXI serial-transfer helpers at `0x802282F0..0x802284AC` (ring index,
//!   object publication, IRQ `0x19` install and the cached identity probe).
//!
//! The `0x80220000` SDP section above and these driver layers are unrelated.
//!
//! Wave 79 added 26 bodies to the `0x80200000` bucket, all of them in the gap
//! between the HCI packet builders of wave 66 and the HID-host table of
//! wave 68:
//!
//! - three HID-host slot helpers at `0x802011D8..0x80201284` (the disconnect
//!   reason getter, the `BD_ADDR`-to-handle lookup and the `u16` slot store)
//!   plus the role-change completion block at `0x80201514`,
//! - the packet-type-mask to ACL-payload-size table at `0x80201570`,
//! - four BTM command-complete handlers at `0x80201AA4..0x802057E8`: RSSI,
//!   link quality, disconnect and inquiry completion,
//! - three small BTM helpers at `0x80205FEC`, `0x802068C4` and `0x80208988`
//!   plus the AFH re-send gate at `0x802089A4`,
//! - the L2CAP/HCI event core at `0x8020AE04..0x8020BAC4` (ten bodies): three
//!   connect/config payload parsers, the opcode dispatch tree
//!   [`fn_8020b14c`], the command-complete and command-status matchers
//!   [`fn_8020b2a4`]/[`fn_8020b5bc`], the status-aware dispatcher
//!   [`fn_8020b3bc`], the timeout redispatch [`fn_8020b6e4`] and the link-key
//!   notification splitter [`fn_8020b83c`],
//! - the HCI `0x040C` address-command builder at `0x8020D244`,
//! - the HID device-pool teardown at `0x8020FE8C`.
//!
//! The two event matchers open with the `0x802DCD74` register-save prologue and
//! are printed without a parameter list; the incoming `r3` survives that thunk
//! untouched, which is how the packet pointer is recovered.
//!
//! Wave 82 added 28 bodies to the same `0x80200000` bucket, closing two
//! contiguous holes in the L2CAP layer:
//!
//! - the four primitive senders at `0x80212C64..0x80212E90` (configuration
//!   request/response, disconnect request/response), the idle-timeout setter
//!   `0x80213058` and the empty callback `0x8021323C`,
//! - the connection-block layer at `0x80214C18..0x80216BC4`: the timeout
//!   handler, the transmit-window accounting, the four-block allocator, lookup
//!   by `BD_ADDR`, the reassembly/segment-complete path, the connection-table
//!   initialiser and the `0x803D4C41` getter,
//! - four helpers reached from that layer: the CCB lookup `0x80217F88`, the
//!   PSM-slot allocator `0x80217FE4`, the disconnect-all `0x802183D0` and the
//!   RFCOMM init `0x8021879C`,
//! - the port-timeout close `0x80219594`, the UIH header builder `0x8021D74C`,
//!   the port timer stop `0x8021E628` and the SDP attribute search `0x8021F08C`.
//!
//! The CCB pool is now named: ten `0x7C`-byte records at `0x803D4DB8` with
//! local channel ids `0x40..0x49`, linked through `+8` and owned by the four
//! connection blocks through `+0x10`.
//!
//! Wave 84 added 25 bodies in the two clusters that waves 60 and 69 had left
//! open in the same `0x80220000` bucket:
//!
//! - the SDP data-element codec at `0x80223390..0x80223F40` (8 bodies): the
//!   attribute-id list builder, the attribute-pair encoder, the error-response
//!   sender, the sequence-of-UUID and sequence-of-id parsers, the size/type
