//! are `// inferred:` from [`fn_802180f8`], which consumes the same offsets.
//! The bucket is now fully translated except for the entries of other lanes.
//!
//! Wave 113 closed the first of the two `0x80220000` entries that wave 108 had
//! recorded as deliberate skips: the text appender `0x8022FA70` (2052 bytes).
//! The body is the draw-side twin of [`fn_8022f350`] measuring pass:
//!
//! - the anchor measure [`MEASURE_80230280`] sets the starting pen and returns
//!   the measured width,
//! - the encoded buffer is walked one character at a time; codes below `0x20`
//!   go through the font object at `+0x60` (slot `+0x10` to test whether the
//!   glyph fits, slot `+0x0C` to draw it and advance the `{object, cursor}`
//!   pair), codes at or above `0x20` add spacing and start a new line through
//!   [`fn_8022d5f0`],
//! - status `3` re-lays the glyph with [`fn_8022f350`] over a sentinel-seeded
//!   copy of the object, status `1`/`2` reset or set the wrap flag and status
//!   `4` jumps to the finish block.
//!
//! The finish block reports `max(measured, pen_x - initial_x)` and restores or
//! advances the pen according to the `+0x5C` anchor bits and the `newline`
//! argument. The five `+0x24`/`+0x28`/`+0x44`/`+0x4C`/`+0x50` spacing fields
//! are named from the literal arithmetic; the two virtual slot roles remain
//! `// inferred:` from their argument shapes.
//!
//! The other entry, the EXI block worker `0x80228644`, had no body then: its
//! export contains twelve `Removing unreachable block` warnings and reduces to
//! an empty `while (1)` loop after the countdown prelude. Wave 116 decoded the
//! intact 186-instruction stream in `work/dol/main.elf` instead and landed the
//! body (see the wave 116 section below).
//!
//! Wave 115 closed the last open entry of the `0x80200000` bucket, the
//! device-database dump `0x80200190` (224 bytes), so all 256 functions of that
//! bucket now have bodies:
//!
//! - the header line is gated on the same `bta_hh` trace level
//!   ([`BTA_HH_LOG_LEVEL`]) as the rest of the HID host and names the dump,
//! - the sixteen `0x20`-byte records at [`BTA_HH_DEV_DB`] are walked in order;
//!   each entry prints the `kdev[%d] in_use[%d] handle[%d]` line and then the
//!   `attr_mask[%04x] state [%d] sub_class[%02x] index = %d` line,
//! - the separator line closes the dump.
//!
//! Wave 99 had skipped the address because the export mixed a `0x10`-element
//! index with a `0x20`-byte index; both are the same `0x20`-byte stride once
//! the `u16` element scaling is accounted for, and `work/dol/strings.txt`
//! supplies the full format strings that name the fields.
//!
//! Wave 116 closed the last open entry of the `0x80220000` bucket, the EXI
//! block worker `0x80228644` (744 bytes), so all 216 functions of that bucket
//! now have bodies:
//!
//! - the third argument is the direction: non-zero packs the caller buffer into
//!   one big-endian word and stores it to [`EXI_DATA_CD006838`], zero reads the
//!   register back and unpacks it with the same shift table,
//! - both directions then write the command word
//!   `TSTART | (write << 2) | ((length - 1) << 4)` to [`EXI_STATUS_CD006834`]
//!   and spin while `TSTART` remains set; the routine always returns `1`,
//! - the eight-byte pack loop and the byte tail OR into one accumulator
//!   (`r0`), which is why `exi_byte_shift` reproduces the wrapped
//!   `(3 - index) * 8` count instead of a plain byte swap.
//!
//! The body comes from the 186-instruction stream in `work/dol/main.elf`: the
//! Ghidra export stops at the `TSTART` poll and drops the remaining twelve
//! blocks as unreachable, which is why waves 108 and 113 skipped it. The four
//! transfer helpers still call the address, as they did before.

use crate::platform;
use crate::game::bases::{interface_call_desc, Descriptor};

/// Calls a PowerPC function with eight integer arguments (`r3`..`r10`).
///
/// `crate::platform` stops at [`platform::call7`]; the `bta_hh_sdp_cmpl`-style
/// diagnostic calls pass the level, a format string and six address bytes, so
/// one stack argument is needed.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr` must point to a function with this ABI.
unsafe fn call8(
    addr: usize,
    a: u32,
    b: u32,
    c: u32,
    d: u32,
    e: u32,
    f: u32,
    g: u32,
    h: u32,
) -> u32 {
    let func = crate::platform::guest_fn!(addr, fn(u32, u32, u32, u32, u32, u32, u32, u32) -> u32);
    func(a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h)
}

